description: Prefer shared consts/enums for repeated values (without overdoing)
alwaysApply: true
---

# Single source of truth — consts & enums

When a string, number, or status is used in more than one place (or will be), put it in a named const/enum/object. Prefer this over scattered magic strings.

## Do

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — shared codes/status
export const ErrorCode = {
  UNAUTHORIZED: 'UNAUTHORIZED',
  TOKEN_EXPIRED: 'TOKEN_EXPIRED',
} as const;

export const HttpStatus = {
  OK: 200,
  UNAUTHORIZED: 401,
} as const;

throw new UnauthorizedError(); // class wires code + status once
```

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — domain roles, cookie names, route prefixes
export const OrgRole = { ADMIN: 'admin', EMPLOYEE: 'employee' } as const;
export const COOKIE_NAME = 'jwt';
```

## Don't overdo

- One-off UI copy, log messages, or a value used once in a single function → leave inline
- Don't invent enums for every prop or boolean
- Don't duplicate the same const in web and api unless you extract a shared package
- Prefer `as const` objects when you need a union type; use `enum` only when it clearly fits the team style (this repo leans `as const`)

## Where to put them

| Kind | Typical home |
|------|----------------|
| HTTP / API error codes | `apps/api/src/constants/` |
| Auth cookie / JWT claims keys | next to tokens util or `constants/` |
| Org roles, issue statuses | shared constants (or `packages/shared` later) |
| Web-only UI keys | `apps/web/lib/` or feature folder |

## Agent habit

Before hardcoding a status code, error code, role, cookie name, or path segment: check for an existing const; if missing and it will be reused (or is part of the public API contract), add one.
